var e=`<p>This is the question behind almost every enquiry we receive, usually phrased as whether the degree will be &quot;accepted&quot;. The answer is settled in principle and conditional in practice, and the condition is the part that matters.</p>
<h2>The established position</h2>
<p>A degree earned through a recognized open and distance or online programme is treated as equivalent to the corresponding degree earned on campus, for employment and for further study. This is the long-standing regulatory position and it is not seriously in dispute.</p>
<p>In practice this means a recruiter cannot disqualify your degree solely because it was earned at a distance, and a university cannot refuse it for postgraduate admission on that ground alone.</p>
<h2>What the equivalence depends on</h2>
<p>Entirely on the programme having been entitled for that mode, at that university, in the session you studied it.</p>
<p>This is worth being blunt about. Equivalence is not a property of distance education in general. It is a property of properly entitled programmes. A degree from a university that was not entitled to offer that programme in that mode is not a lesser degree, it is a degree with a problem, and no general statement about equivalence repairs it.</p>
<p>So the verification you do before enrolling is the thing that makes the equivalence real.</p>
<h2>Where equivalence does not settle the matter</h2>
<p>Eligibility for a specific post follows that recruiter&#39;s own notification. If a notification requires a particular degree, or a particular discipline, or specifies something about the mode, that requirement governs. Equivalence means your degree is not excluded for being earned at a distance. It does not override a written criterion.</p>
<p>Some regulated professions and some foreign jurisdictions apply their own rules, particularly where licensure is involved. If your plan depends on practising in a regulated field or studying abroad, check that body&#39;s own position rather than relying on the general principle.</p>
<h2>What to keep</h2>
<p>When you graduate, keep the degree certificate, the consolidated marksheet, and evidence of the university&#39;s recognition and the programme&#39;s entitlement for your enrolment session, together. Verification can happen years later at a promotion or a visa application, and assembling that evidence retrospectively is unpleasant.</p>
<h2>If someone tells you otherwise</h2>
<p>Occasionally an employer or an institution asserts that a distance degree does not count. Ask for the requirement in writing. Frequently it turns out to be a specific criterion in a notification rather than a blanket position, and the specific criterion is something you can plan around.</p>
<p>Send us the requirement and we will tell you what satisfies it.</p>
`;export{e as html};